diagnosis_text:
The symptom reported in the ticket — laptop on 172.16.20.x unable to reach
workstations and printers on 172.16.10.x — cannot be reproduced in the live
lab. Full bidirectional inter-VLAN reachability is confirmed between all hosts
on both subnets.

The inter-VLAN routing path works as follows: mswitch1 acts purely as a
Layer-2 VLAN bridge, presenting VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 as two separate bridge
domains (br_vlan10 and br_vlan20) to router0 via 802.1Q-tagged trunk
subinterfaces (eth0.10 and eth0.20). Router0 terminates both VLANs with
gateway addresses 172.16.10.1/24 and 172.16.20.1/24 and performs inter-VLAN
routing between them at Layer 3. IP forwarding is enabled on router0, and the
routing table carries direct connected routes for both subnets.

Diagnostic probes confirmed: laptop0 (172.16.20.10) reaches every 172.16.10.x
host — gateway (172.16.10.1), pc4 (172.16.10.4), pc5 (172.16.10.5), laptop2
(172.16.10.12), and laptop3 (172.16.10.13) — all with 0% packet loss and
TTL=63 (one router hop via router0, as expected). The reverse path from pc4
back to laptop0 is equally healthy. There are no iptables or ebtables filter
rules anywhere on the path. The FDB on mswitch1 has learned the correct MAC
addresses on the correct VLAN bridge members.

Root cause: No fault is present; the 172.16.20.x ↔ 172.16.10.x inter-VLAN
path is fully operational. The user's ticket does not reflect a real network
outage at the time of this investigation.

Corrective action: No network change is required. Re-engage the user to verify
whether the issue has self-resolved, confirm they are using the correct source
IP (172.16.20.10 on laptop0), and rule out a client-side cause such as a stale
ARP cache, misconfigured host firewall, or a temporary service disruption that
has since cleared.
=== END ===
